\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Eighteen hexagons through every edge: the unique graph of order thirty-two}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{theorem}\label{main}
The minimum order of a finite simple quartic graph of girth six with
exactly eighteen hexagons through every edge is thirty-two. There is
exactly one such graph of order thirty-two, up to isomorphism.
\end{theorem}
This recovers the lower bound and exact count in the $\lambda=18$ row
of Table~2 in Goedgebeur--Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of
edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, physical page~18.
The retained source graph is \texttt{UntilOrder250\_205}. The argument
uses the acquired local equations and partial-matching forest theorem,
with a new observation which makes a second outside boundary unnecessary.

\section{The entire outside set is visible}
Fix an edge $uv$ in a hypothetical graph. The two rooted trees of depth
two obtained by excluding $uv$ at the first step have twenty-six distinct
vertices. An identification or an extra edge within one side produces
a cycle of length at most five. Thus the only extra edges among these
vertices join opposite leaves. There are three branches of three leaves
on each side. Edges between any pair of opposite branches form a
matching, since two neighbours in the same branch produce a quadrangle.
Every cross edge gives one hexagon through $uv$, and every such hexagon
is obtained in this way. There are therefore eighteen cross edges.
Their nine matching sizes are $3-d_{ij}$, where
\[
 0\le d_{ij}\le3,\qquad \sum_{i,j=0}^2d_{ij}=9.                 \tag{1}
\]
There are nine unfinished leaf incidences on each side. Every one must
meet an outside vertex: another cross edge would change the count
through $uv$.

\begin{lemma}\label{outside}
If the graph has at most thirty-two vertices, the complement $Y$ of
the twenty-six Moore vertices has five or six vertices. Each vertex
of $Y$ has a neighbour among the Moore leaves. The induced graph on
$Y$ has one edge if $|Y|=5$ and three edges if $|Y|=6$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Put $t=|Y|\le6$. Summing degrees in $Y$ and using the eighteen edges
from the Moore vertices gives
\[
                  2e(Y)=4t-18.                               \tag{2}
\]
Nonnegativity forces $t\ge5$ and gives the two edge counts. If a vertex
of $Y$ had no Moore neighbour, all four of its neighbours would lie
in $Y$, forcing $e(Y)\ge4$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}
For five outside vertices the induced graph is one edge and three
isolated vertices. For six it is one of
\[
 3K_2,\qquad P_3+K_2+K_1,\qquad P_4+2K_1,\qquad K_{1,3}+2K_1. \tag{3}
\]
Indeed, a graph with three edges and no triangle is a forest, and the
list follows by distributing its three edges among its tree components.
The program need not assume a type from (3); it completes every possible
outside edge set directly.

\section{Root configurations and exact outside partitions}
We recall the acquired local equations in their precise present form.
For a side profile $P$ of positive unfinished degrees, define
\(f(P)=(27-\sum_{s\in P}s^2)/2\). Let $b(P)$ be the maximum of
$\sum_B\binom{|B|}{2}$ over partitions of its nine distinguished
incidences into parts of size at most three, with neither a repeated
leaf in a part nor a repeated pair of leaves in different parts.
The necessary inequalities are
\[
                  f(P_L)\le b(P_R),\qquad f(P_R)\le b(P_L).     \tag{4}
\]
For completeness, an unfinished degree $s$ at a right leaf supplies
$\binom{3-s}{2}$ common-neighbour hexagons between left branches.
There are eighteen hexagons through $u$ which do not use $uv$.
Subtracting the supplied ones leaves
$18-\sum_{i=1}^9\binom{3-s_i}{2}=(27-\sum_i s_i^2)/2$.
Outside common neighbours of left leaves can supply at most $b(P_L)$.
Interchanging sides proves (4). Ignoring the branch condition when
computing $b$ only increases the bound. The twelve exact maxima are
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrrrrrr}
P&1^9&2,1^7&3,1^6&2^2,1^5&3,2,1^4&2^3,1^3&3^2,1^3&3,2^2,1^2&2^4,1&3^2,2,1&3,2^3&3^3\\\hline
b(P)&9&9&9&9&7&9&6&7&7&5&6&3
\end{array}                                                    \tag{5}
\]
A first-appearance recursion computes them; a separate implementation
checks all $21\,147$ partitions of nine distinguished positions for
each profile.

Write $L_0,L_1,L_2$ for the left branches and $a_i$ for their cross-edge
counts. Once their leaves have been saturated, the root-pair equations
are, for distinct $i,j,k$,
\[
              \sum_w |N(w)\cap L_i|\,|N(w)\cap L_j|=a_k.       \tag{6}
\]
If $b_{ij}$ counts hexagons through the two root edges in branches
$i,j$, then $a_i+b_{ij}+b_{ik}=18$ and $a_0+a_1+a_2=18$.
Solving gives $b_{ij}=a_k$. Common neighbours of the leaves give
exactly these hexagons; girth six ensures that their paths are simple.
The same equations hold on the right. These are the acquired
root-pair identities, not an assumption about the final graph.

There are $13\,051$ matrices in (1), in $228$ orbits under row
permutations, column permutations and transpose. Both the preparer
and the C++ verifier independently construct this entire integer set.
These symmetries are actual symmetries of the two rooted trees.
For each matrix, apply the acquired partial-matching forest theorem
to the six sets of three leaves. In a forest block of matching size
$k$, directed from parent to child, retain each parent $k$-subset
matched in order to the first $k$ child labels. A fresh child
permutation achieves this form and acts on all incident blocks;
earlier forest blocks remain fixed. Induction away from the forest
roots proves coverage. Every block outside the forest retains all
partial injective matchings. Reverse orientations use the transposed
prescription. This is an orbit cover, not a uniqueness assertion.

The chosen forests give $51\,869\,700$ normalized product choices.
The traversal rejects a prefix only for a newly created quadrangle;
the partial graph is bipartite. At complete assignments it tests (4).
Every subsequent reuse has a checked literal bijection preserving
the unordered root edge and every adjacency row. Nauty supplies a
lookup suggestion and a permutation only. A false merge fails the
check; a false split merely creates another case to process.
The complete run visits $19\,336\,649$ prefixes and $7\,310\,487$
girth-admissible assignments. Its $6\,797\,839$ checked maps retain
$398\,537$ root graphs.

For each root graph, partition the nine left incidences by their
outside neighbour, inserting each incidence into every permissible
existing part or a new part. Lemma~\ref{outside} permits rejection
as soon as there are more than six parts. A part contains no repeated
leaf and at most one leaf from each branch. Leaf pairs may neither
repeat among parts nor already have an old common neighbour.
These are necessary quadrangle restrictions. New contributions to
(6) may not exceed its right-hand side; at the end equality is required.
There is no monotonicity assumption beyond these nonnegative counts.
Repeat the construction on the right. Every actual graph supplies
one of the resulting pairs of partitions.

An outside vertex meeting both sides corresponds to a pair of parts,
one on each side. Enumerate every partial one-to-one matching between
the two sets of parts, permitting a match only if its combined degree
is at most four. Unmatched parts are distinct outside vertices.
The number of current vertices cannot decrease as right parts are
processed, so a partial join with more than six outside vertices
cannot be completed within the order bound. Every full join is checked
for girth. Later reuse is by an unrooted literal isomorphism: all
Moore vertices are saturated, and the remaining problem depends only
on the partial graph and its vertex degrees, not on the chosen root.

The complete side search has $2\,260\,809$ partitions; $5\,407$ roots
have a partition on both sides. There are $648\,732\,191$ terminal
joins within the order bound; $648\,731\,580$ have a short cycle.
The remaining $611$ literal maps retain $66$ partial graphs. The
additional $421\,884\,142$ over-order rejections are prefixes of the
join recursion, not further terminal choices.

\section{Completion and the source graph}
By Lemma~\ref{outside}, every vertex of a desired graph is already
present. The only remaining edges join deficient outside vertices.
Choose the first deficient vertex $x$ and enumerate every subset of
later deficient vertices of size $4-\deg(x)$. Add its edges in order,
rejecting any edge whose endpoints already have distance less than
five. Earlier vertices are saturated, so this enumerates every simple
quartic completion. Backtracking restores every adjacency row.
The recursion has at most six outside vertices and adds one or three
edges in total. No vertices or untested edges remain afterwards.

Five of the $66$ partial graphs already have more than eighteen
hexagons on an edge and are impossible. The others yield four
quartic graphs of girth at least six. Three have the wrong count on
an edge. The remaining graph has thirty-two vertices and exactly
eighteen hexagons on each of its sixty-four edges. Counts are obtained
by enumerating every simple alternate path of length five; stopping
a count after it exceeds eighteen is valid for rejection. Its literal
bijection to \texttt{UntilOrder250\_205} is recorded and checked on
all $1\,024$ ordered vertex pairs. The source graph's degrees, girth
and sixty-four edge counts are checked separately.

A second program enumerates all $30\,030$ possible outside-edge subsets
of the $66$ retained graphs, independently of the vertex recursion.
Of these, $990$ satisfy the degrees and $986$ have a short cycle.
It recovers the same four completions and the same single positive.
This check also tests the five early overflow rejections. Independent
Bell-partition checks compare the actual side recursion on twenty-three
root samples. They supplement the written coverage proof and the
complete closed execution; sampling supplies no missing census premise.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
Every graph of order at most thirty-two supplies a covered Moore
configuration, outside partitions and a join. Each discarded branch
violates a necessary condition. Lemma~\ref{outside} shows that the
finite completion step includes every edge of every remaining graph.
Every reuse is justified by a literal isomorphism. The only final graph
is the checked source graph of order thirty-two. Thus no smaller graph
exists and the graph at the minimum is unique.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and scope.}
The minimum and uniqueness are source results. The earlier components
supply the Moore-tree and root-pair identities, partial-matching forest
normalization, and literal-map architecture. The outside-degree lemma,
its application to a complete first outside set, the bounded completion
and its exact new executions are the additional work here. The author
also authored the nineteen-hexagon component and previously reviewed
the twenty-hexagon component; distinct review of this proof is recorded
separately. No priority, newly solved conjecture, whole-original recovery,
Lean or compression-factor claim is made.
